{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices for most of the horizon (often ~+1 to +3 €/t). Apply an upward level shift, especially when the last observed price is in the mid-60s and the recent trend is up.",
    "Underestimates rallies / momentum: after an upswing in the last 5–10 days, the model fails to carry momentum forward (true path keeps rising while forecast flattens/declines). Add a positive trend term proportional to the recent slope (e.g., 5–10d return) for the first ~15–20 forecast days.",
    "Forecasts peak too early and too low: in rising regimes, the model’s local maxima occur earlier than realized and at lower amplitude. Delay and lift peaks (shift the forecast profile slightly forward and increase peak height).",
    "Overstates late-horizon drawdowns: beyond ~20 days, the model often mean-reverts downward too aggressively (sometimes projecting sharp drops) while realized prices stay elevated or only gently revert. Damp mean reversion in the back half of the horizon; cap negative drift unless recent history shows sustained decline.",
    "Asymmetry in mean reversion: model reverts down faster than the market does after strength; use slower downward reversion speed than upward continuation (i.e., bullish regimes persist longer than forecast).",
    "Spike handling: when the model projects a sharp late drop after a high level, treat it as overshoot—replace with a smoother path anchored to recent realized range (e.g., keep prices within ±1–2σ of last-30d distribution unless strong downtrend evidence)."
  ]
}
